{"id":"chatcmpl-2dc3ee15-ea2f-4b71-aba2-c86d68dfc3c6","object":"chat.completion","created":1795246573,"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"","content":"```json\n{\n  \"highlights\": \"This task demonstrates a cross-validation strategy for medical image interpretation: when a single model's reading may be unreliable, query multiple independent expert models and require agreement before committing to an answer. The key insight is that laterality and subtle findings (like small effusions) benefit from redundant, independently-phrased queries. The final answer is justified by convergence of evidence rather than a single inference.\",\n  \"patterns\": [\n    \"When a visual finding is subtle or laterality-dependent, query two or more independent expert models and only answer when their conclusions converge.\",\n    \"Rephrase the same diagnostic question across tools (e.g., asking about angle blunting vs. meniscus sign) to reduce phrasing-specific bias and surface consistent evidence.\",\n    \"Use a domain-specialist tool first to establish the finding, then a general vision-language model to cross-check, treating agreement as confidence.\",\n    \"Ground the final answer in the specific anatomical evidence cited by the models (e.g., costophrenic angle blunting) rather than the model's label alone.\"\n  ],\n  \"playbooks\": {\n    \"Medical imaging QA\": \"Prefer multi-model consensus for subtle or lateralized findings; ask each model to describe the anatomical sign, not just the diagnosis.\",\n    \"Binary/multiple-choice visual diagnosis\": \"Convert the choice into an open diagnostic question per tool, then map the described evidence back to the options.\",\n    \"High-stakes classification\": \"Require at least two independent sources to agree before selecting an answer; document the shared evidence in the rationale.\"\n  },\n  \"checklists\": [\n    \"Confirm at least two independent tools/models report the same finding and laterality before answering.\",\n    \"Verify the cited anatomical evidence (e.g., posterior costophrenic angle blunting) actually supports the chosen option over its alternative.\"\n  ]\n}\n```"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":492,"total_tokens":876,"completion_tokens":384,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":492},"completion_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":384}}}